## How a Doctor Actually Confirms Which One You Have

You sit down in front of a dermatologist after weeks of guessing, and the first thing that should happen is not a glance and a prescription pad, it's a nail clipping. The gold-standard test for toenail fungus is periodic acid-Schiff staining of nail clippings, or a KOH, potassium hydroxide, preparation. A KOH preparation is a laboratory test in which nail debris is dissolved in KOH solution and examined under a microscope for fungal elements, and it is a rapid, inexpensive first-line diagnostic tool for onychomycosis. A negative result argues strongly against fungal infection, which on its own narrows the diagnosis considerably.

Psoriasis diagnosis works differently. It is largely clinical, built on nail appearance, pitting pattern, oil drop signs, and the presence of skin or joint symptoms elsewhere on the body, sometimes supported by the Nail Psoriasis Severity Index that dermatologists use to score how far the condition has progressed. If you are heading into an appointment, ask specifically for a KOH prep or PAS stain by name rather than accepting a visual diagnosis alone, since that single request is what separates a confirmed answer from another educated guess. Once fungal infection is properly ruled in or out, <a href='/blog/toenail-fungus/toenail-fungus-home-remedies'>home remedies for toenail fungus</a> may support treatment alongside prescription options, but only once you actually know that is what you are treating.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## Get The Right Answer Before You Treat Anything

A discoloured nail is not a diagnosis, it is a question, and the only people who answer it correctly without a lab test are the ones who know exactly which clues are decisive. Pitting and the oil drop sign point one way. Odour and a single spreading nail point the other. Both at once point toward co-occurrence, which is more common than either condition's own marketing material admits.

If your nail has not budged after weeks of antifungal cream, stop guessing and start asking your GP for a KOH prep by name. That one sentence does more for your nail than another month of the wrong tube of cream ever will. Get the test, get the right diagnosis, and treat the condition you actually have rather than the one you assumed.
